meta {
  name: 03 Refresh while blocked
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/refresh
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "refreshToken": "{{studentRefreshToken}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 201) {
    bru.setEnvVar("studentAccessToken", res.getBody().accessToken);
    bru.setEnvVar("studentRefreshToken", res.getBody().refreshToken);
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
}

docs {
  Refresh KEEPS WORKING while blocked. That is a decision, not an oversight.

  Blocking it would strand the student: their tokens would expire, they could
  not get new ones, and the moment their center pays they would be locked out
  of an account that is now perfectly valid. They keep a live session; the
  learning routes are what refuse them.

  ## Blocked response

  ```json
  {
    "accessToken": "...",
    "refreshToken": "...",
    "subscription": { "status": "BLOCKED", "studentsMayLearn": false }
  }
  ```

  201, with the bad news attached. A client that refreshes in the background
  learns it is blocked here, rather than from a 403 on whatever the student
  happened to tap next.

  ## Refresh tokens are single use

  Run this TWICE without re-running `01 Student login`.

  The second attempt must fail with 401. The token rotates on every use and the
  old one dies, so a stolen refresh token is only useful until the real client
  next refreshes — at which point one of the two is caught.

  The post-response script above captures the new pair, so a single run leaves
  you in a working state. If you get an unexpected 401 here, that is usually
  because you ran it twice; re-run `01 Student login`.

  ## After restoring the subscription

  Same request, and `subscription.status` follows the database immediately.
  Nothing is cached between these two calls.
}
